    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's what I implemented:\n\n**The change:** On a `customerCode` collision with an existing owner, append `-<n>` with the smallest `n \u2265 2` that makes it unique, and store that de-duplicated code.\n\n**Where I put each piece** (keeping single responsibilities):\n\n1. **`CustomerCode.dedupe(candidate, existing)`** \u2014 a new pure helper alongside the existing pure `CustomerCode` functions. It returns the candidate unchanged when there's no collision, otherwise scans `n = 2, 3, \u2026` for the first free suffix. This is the reusable, testable core logic.\n\n2. **`DeduplicateCustomerCode`** \u2014 a new one-method OfficeFloor function class that gathers existing owners' codes from `OwnerRepository` and applies `CustomerCode.dedupe`, mutating the built owner in place (mirroring `CountNamesakes`/`AssignCustomerCode`). I kept this separate from `AssignCustomerCode` deliberately \u2014 that class's contract is \"does not depend on other owners,\" and folding collision resolution in would break that and turn it into a catch-all.\n\n3. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new step in between `assignCustomerCode` and `assignHousehold`, under the same `transaction` governance, so it runs after the code is computed and before `SaveOwner` (so the not-yet-persisted owner can't collide with itself).",
